\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}
\label{sec:why_kstar}
\label{sec:one_wash}
\label{sec:negative_graduated}
\label{sec:repr_dependence}
\label{sec:boundary_cases}
\label{sec:length_confound}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_jsd_decay.pdf}
\caption{Token-level JSD between adjacent depth pairs on log scale. Blue band: min-max range across 7 C4 models with $\Kempirical{=}2$; dashed purple: Mistral-7B ($\Kempirical{=}1$, cross-scorer validated); green: arXiv Pythia ($\Kempirical{=}3$); dotted red: retrain-chain ($\Kempirical{\geq}4$). C4 models exhibit a sharp cliff ($\lambda \approx 2.2$--$3.5$), while arXiv decays gradually ($\lambda \approx 1.0$) and retrain-chain shows no cliff ($\lambda = 0.285$).}
\label{fig:jsd_decay}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{One-wash laundering (web text).}
For general web text, $\Kempirical{=}2$ reflects concentrated information loss: 0v1 is near-trivially separable ($\auc \geq 0.92$), 1v2 drops by ${\sim}$30\,pp, and 2v3 falls below $\theta$.
A single generation pass renders text indistinguishable from multiply recycled content, limiting depth-aware curation to a binary boundary.
Converging evidence supports a data-intrinsic interpretation: OBD achieves 96\% depth-0 recall but only 14--22\% for depths 2--4; frontier LLMs show 8--38\% (McNemar $p > 0.05$ vs.\ OBD; Table~\ref{tab:s3_llm_zeroshot}).

\paragraph{JSD decay and the discrimination boundary.}
Token-level JSD between adjacent generations reveals two distinct decay regimes (Figure~\ref{fig:jsd_decay}): C4 models exhibit a sharp cliff (0v1 JSD exceeds 1v2 by 6--56$\times$, median ${\sim}$15$\times$) followed by rapid convergence to a noise floor, while arXiv decays ${\sim}$3$\times$ slower ($\lambda \approx 1.0$ vs.\ $2.9$).
Cross-model consistency within each domain indicates that the contraction rate of $G_\delta$ is governed by domain structure rather than model architecture; the retrain-chain exhibits a fundamentally different profile ($\lambda = 0.285$, no sharp cliff), reflecting model-adaptation artifacts rather than text-intrinsic loss.
Detailed decay rates and domain comparisons appear in Appendix~\ref{sec:jsd_extended}.

\paragraph{Graduated filtering failure.}
For web text, 2v3 $\auc < \theta$ across all model configurations, so depth assignments for $d \geq 2$ approximate random labels; graduated weighting injects noise, explaining convergence toward binary at high $\alpha$ (Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_limitations}).
DSIR fails differently: bigram hashing captures domain shift but not depth-correlated signatures.
This pattern persists across contamination ratios (10--50\%; Appendix~\ref{sec:dose_response}), indicating the failure is structural rather than severity-dependent.

\paragraph{Representation dependence.}
$\Kempirical$ is representation-dependent: frozen Qwen embeddings yield $\Kempirical^{\text{neural}} = 3$, while Pythia and OLMo match the 15D boundary at $\Kempirical^{\text{neural}} = 2$ (Table~\ref{tab:neural_probe_crossmodel}).
The 15D features trade one depth level of sensitivity for cross-model consistency (7/8 configurations $\Kempirical{\leq}2$), making them the preferred operational choice.
Cross-scorer validation is consistent with $\Kempirical$ being governed by the generation operator rather than the scoring model ($\rho > 0.90$ feature ranking preservation; length confound ruled out in Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_limitations}), though $\Kempirical^{\text{neural}}{=}3$ for Qwen indicates that representation choice can modulate the boundary.

\paragraph{Domain dependence of $\Kempirical$.}
C4 web text consistently yields $\Kempirical{=}2$ (7/8 model configurations), while structured academic text (arXiv) yields $\Kempirical{=}3$ across five models spanning four architecture families (Section~\ref{sec:cross_domain}).
Domain-specific stylistic constraints amplify per-generation distributional shift, making contamination in structured corpora trackable to greater depth.
With scorer confound controlled (same-family Qwen scoring inflates $\Kempirical$ by +1 on Wikipedia), all six models yield $\Kempirical{=}1$ on Wikipedia (Appendix~\ref{sec:wiki_domain}), producing a uniform domain ordering: arXiv ($\Kempirical{=}3$) $>$ C4 ($\Kempirical{=}2$) $>$ Wikipedia ($\Kempirical{=}1$).
Source-domain structural complexity is the sole factor that systematically shifts $\Kempirical$ across all tested model configurations.

\paragraph{Prompt-chain vs.\ retrain-chain $\Kempirical$.}
Retrain-chain experiments (Pythia-1.4B LoRA) yield $\Kempirical{\geq}4$ (Table~\ref{tab:retrain_ci}), far exceeding the prompt-chain boundary of $\Kempirical{=}2$.
The two boundaries address distinct operational questions: prompt-chain $\Kempirical{=}2$ measures text-level information decay (the relevant boundary for data curation on wild web text), while retrain-chain $\Kempirical{\geq}4$ reflects model-adaptation artifacts that accumulate across fine-tuning iterations (Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_limitations}).

\paragraph{Boundary cases and scale effect.}
Of 8 C4 configurations, 7 yield $\Kempirical{=}2$; Mistral-7B ($\Kempirical{=}1$; 1v2 $\auc = 0.594$) is the sole exception, validating $\theta{=}0.60$ as an effective discriminator.
Mistral's cross-domain profile ($\Kempirical{=}3$ on arXiv despite $\Kempirical{=}1$ on C4) reinforces the domain-governed interpretation; scale trends and detailed boundary analysis appear in Appendix~\ref{sec:boundary_extended}.

\paragraph{Scorer sensitivity.}
$\Kempirical$ can depend on scorer family: on Wikipedia, Qwen generators scored by Qwen-1.5B yield $\Kempirical{=}2$, but cross-family scoring with Pythia-1.4B reduces this to $\Kempirical{=}1$ ($\Delta = -2.4$\,pp; Appendix~\ref{sec:wiki_domain}).
On C4, cross-scorer validation preserves feature rankings ($\rho > 0.90$) and $\Kempirical$ for 6/8 configurations (Section~\ref{sec:transfer}).
Scorer sensitivity is thus domain-dependent and inversely related to domain structural complexity: arXiv (3/4 generators preserve $\Kempirical{=}3$) is more robust than Wikipedia (0/2 retain $\Kempirical{=}2$).
We recommend cross-family scorer validation as a standard robustness check for $\Kempirical$ claims on new domains.

\paragraph{Practical implications.}
$\Kempirical$ translates directly into curation policy.
For web text ($\Kempirical{=}2$), graduated depth weighting beyond this boundary injects noise, validating binary filtering as optimal (Section~\ref{sec:filtering}); for arXiv ($\Kempirical{=}3$), an additional depth level carries discriminative signal that binary filtering discards.
The domain ordering (arXiv $>$ C4 $>$ Wikipedia) implies that mixed-domain corpora require domain-stratified filtering rather than a uniform depth threshold.

\section{Application: \texorpdfstring{$\Kstar$}{K*}-Aware Data Filtering}
\label{sec:filtering}

$\Kempirical = 2$ makes a falsifiable prediction: binary curation should suffice, and graduated performance should converge toward binary as the decay steepness increases.
We compare six strategies on 30{,}000 documents (depths 0--5): (i)~\textbf{no-filter}, (ii)~\textbf{binary-ours} ($w = 1 - P(\text{synthetic})$ from 15D LightGBM), (iii)~\textbf{binary-Drayson}~\citep{drayson2025}, (iv)~\textbf{graduated} ($w = \exp(-\alpha \cdot \hat{d})$, $\alpha \in \{0.1,\ldots,0.9\}$), (v)~\textbf{surprisal} (retain above median), and (vi)~\textbf{DSIR}~\citep{xie2023dsir}.
We fine-tune Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct via LoRA (rank~8, $\alpha_{\text{LoRA}}{=}16$) for 3 epochs on MMLU and HellaSwag across 5 seeds (51 total runs: 5 base strategies $\times$ 5 seeds $+$ 5 graduated-$\alpha$ values $\times$ 5 seeds $+$ 1 base-model reference; this 100\% synthetic setting is an upper bound~\cite{shumailov2024collapse, dohmatob2024model}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Downstream task performance (MMLU, HellaSwag) under six data-filtering strategies. Binary-ours uses the 15D LightGBM depth classifier; Graduated applies exponentially decaying weights $w = \exp(-\alpha \hat{d})$. Mean $\pm$ std over 5 seeds. Full $\alpha$ sweep in Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_limitations}.}
\label{tab:tab_filtering_main}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{\input{tables/tab_filtering_main}}
\end{table}

Binary-ours minimizes MMLU degradation most effectively (Table~\ref{tab:tab_filtering_main}), reducing the gap from $-$1.53\,pp (no-filter) to $-$0.77\,pp (${\sim}$50\% reduction; $p_{\text{corr}} < 0.05$ vs.\ all alternatives).
These effects are measured in a 100\% synthetic upper-bound setting; real-world contamination ratios are typically lower, and effect sizes would scale accordingly.
The strategy ordering is interpretable through $\Kempirical$: binary filtering exploits the one reliable boundary (0v1), while graduated strategies assign weights to depths 2--5 based on near-random classifier outputs, injecting noise.
Graduated filtering converges monotonically toward binary: at $\alpha{=}0.9$, it matches binary-ours on HellaSwag exactly ($\Delta = 0$, $p_{\text{corr}} = 1.00$) but remains significantly worse on MMLU ($p_{\text{corr}} = 0.011$).
DSIR is indistinguishable from no-filter, confirming that depth-aware features are necessary for curation in recursively contaminated corpora (Figure~\ref{fig:filtering_strategies} in Appendix).
Binary filtering retains a consistent advantage across contamination levels (10--50\%), with effect sizes diminishing 3--6$\times$ relative to the 100\% setting; graduated weighting remains indistinguishable from no-filter at all tested ratios, confirming that depth assignments beyond $\Kempirical$ inject noise regardless of severity (Appendix~\ref{sec:dose_response}).

